1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,956
There are three sensible ways through this, and picking one deliberately beats

2
00:00:04,956 --> 00:00:06,492
drifting through in order.

3
00:00:06,492 --> 00:00:10,980
The full build is every section, in order, with the exercises actually done.

4
00:00:10,980 --> 00:00:15,881
That's about twelve hours including the work, and it's what I'd recommend if the AI

5
00:00:15,881 --> 00:00:17,948
part of your role is going to grow.

6
00:00:17,948 --> 00:00:21,846
The ship-it path is for people with a feature in flight right now.

7
00:00:21,846 --> 00:00:24,444
Sections zero, three, four, seven and eight.

8
00:00:24,444 --> 00:00:29,168
That's spec, evaluation, cost and risk: everything you need to get something out

9
00:00:29,168 --> 00:00:30,940
safely, and nothing you don't.

10
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:34,247
The credibility path is sections one, two, five and six.

11
00:00:34,247 --> 00:00:39,089
Take that if your immediate problem is holding your own in technical conversations

12
00:00:39,089 --> 00:00:41,688
rather than shipping something this quarter.

13
00:00:41,688 --> 00:00:46,530
Which of the three sounds like you right now: the full build, the ship-it path, or

14
00:00:46,530 --> 00:00:47,770
the credibility path?

15
00:00:47,770 --> 00:00:52,494
Pick honestly, not aspirationally, because the route you'd like to have time for

16
00:00:52,494 --> 00:00:54,679
isn't always the one you actually do.

17
00:00:54,679 --> 00:00:56,924
You can always come back for the rest.

18
00:00:56,924 --> 00:01:01,825
Nothing later depends on you having watched everything earlier, with one exception.

19
00:01:01,825 --> 00:01:03,065
Don't skip Section 4.

20
00:01:03,065 --> 00:01:04,423
Everything leans on it.

21
00:01:04,973 --> 00:01:09,271
Before the artifacts, here's the shape of the whole course on one screen.

22
00:01:09,271 --> 00:01:12,038
You start at Section zero, which you're in now.

23
00:01:12,038 --> 00:01:15,923
Sections one and two give you the mental model and the vocabulary.

24
00:01:15,923 --> 00:01:18,396
Section three is where you write the spec.

25
00:01:18,396 --> 00:01:21,280
Then look at Section four, sitting in the middle.

26
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:23,399
That's evaluation, and it's the hub.

27
00:01:23,399 --> 00:01:27,579
Everything to the right of it consumes the measurement you build there.

28
00:01:27,579 --> 00:01:30,405
RAG and agents in five and six are judged by it.

29
00:01:30,405 --> 00:01:32,348
The cost work in seven prices it.

30
00:01:32,348 --> 00:01:34,644
The risk work in eight stress-tests it.

31
00:01:34,644 --> 00:01:36,998
The analytics in nine and ten report it.

32
00:01:36,998 --> 00:01:39,471
That's why I said don't skip Section four.

33
00:01:39,471 --> 00:01:43,474
It isn't the hardest section, but it's the one the others plug into.

34
00:01:44,024 --> 00:01:47,088
Here's the first half of what you'll walk out with.

35
00:01:47,088 --> 00:01:51,113
The PRD template is the one most people tell me changed their week.

36
00:01:51,113 --> 00:01:55,619
It's a spec format built for features that don't behave the same way twice.

37
00:01:55,619 --> 00:02:00,606
The golden dataset spec and the rubric are how you pick the fifty cases that decide

38
00:02:00,606 --> 00:02:04,811
your launch, and how you get two reviewers to score them the same way.

39
00:02:04,811 --> 00:02:07,094
Then two versions of the eval harness.

40
00:02:07,094 --> 00:02:12,081
One is a spreadsheet, so you can build it this afternoon without asking engineering

41
00:02:12,081 --> 00:02:12,862
for anything.

42
00:02:12,862 --> 00:02:17,128
The other is about thirty lines of Python, so the same measurement runs

43
00:02:17,128 --> 00:02:18,930
automatically on every change.

44
00:02:18,930 --> 00:02:21,033
Take whichever fits your situation.

45
00:02:21,033 --> 00:02:23,015
Most people start with the sheet.

46
00:02:23,565 --> 00:02:28,264
The second half is the part that tends to get people promoted, because it's the part

47
00:02:28,264 --> 00:02:30,501
almost nobody else on the team is doing.

48
00:02:30,501 --> 00:02:34,976
The retrieval scorecard tells you why answers are wrong, not just that they are,

49
00:02:34,976 --> 00:02:37,717
which turns a vague complaint into a fixable one.

50
00:02:37,717 --> 00:02:42,416
The agent scoping sheet stops you committing to automation that the maths says can't

51
00:02:42,416 --> 00:02:42,695
work.

52
00:02:42,695 --> 00:02:47,394
The unit economics model answers the question your finance team will eventually ask,

53
00:02:47,394 --> 00:02:50,247
in the meeting where you don't want to be guessing.

54
00:02:50,247 --> 00:02:53,323
The risk register is the one that saves whole quarters.

55
00:02:53,323 --> 00:02:56,232
Bring it to legal at kickoff and you get conditions.

56
00:02:56,232 --> 00:02:58,637
Bring it in launch week and you get a hold.

57
00:02:58,637 --> 00:03:03,280
And the metric tree is the one you'll actually keep using longest, because it isn't

58
00:03:03,280 --> 00:03:04,902
a pre-launch document at all.

59
00:03:04,902 --> 00:03:09,265
One North Star and a couple of branches is what tells you honestly whether the

60
00:03:09,265 --> 00:03:13,181
feature earned its keep six months in, not just on the day it shipped.

61
00:03:13,731 --> 00:03:15,026
Two practical things.

62
00:03:15,026 --> 00:03:18,974
Captions are on every lecture, so if you're a skimmer, use them.

63
00:03:18,974 --> 00:03:22,059
More importantly, bring one real feature with you.

64
00:03:22,059 --> 00:03:26,994
Every exercise in this course is written so you can do it against something from

65
00:03:26,994 --> 00:03:29,339
your own job instead of a toy example.

66
00:03:29,339 --> 00:03:34,212
Pick the AI feature you're most unsure about right now, keep it in mind, and by

67
00:03:34,212 --> 00:03:39,271
Section 8 you'll have specced, measured, costed and risk-assessed it without doing

68
00:03:39,271 --> 00:03:40,196
any extra work.

69
00:03:40,196 --> 00:03:45,193
If you genuinely don't have one, use the support-summarisation case from the next

70
00:03:45,193 --> 00:03:45,687
lecture.

71
00:03:45,687 --> 00:03:48,093
It runs all the way through the course.

72
00:03:48,643 --> 00:03:49,004
Right.

73
00:03:49,004 --> 00:03:50,449
That's the housekeeping.

74
00:03:50,449 --> 00:03:53,218
The next lecture is the one that matters most.

75
00:03:53,218 --> 00:03:57,854
It's six minutes, it's a single idea, and everything else in this course is a

76
00:03:57,854 --> 00:03:58,938
consequence of it.

77
00:03:58,938 --> 00:03:59,480
Let's go.
